Paper text:
Hierarchical Epsilon-Net Graphs: Time Guarantees for HNSW in
Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search
Mohsen Dehghankar
University of Illinois Chicago
Chicago, IL, USA
mdehgh2@uic.eduAbolfazl Asudeh
University of Illinois Chicago
Chicago, IL, USA
asudeh@uic.edu
Abstract
Hierarchical graph-based algorithms such as HNSW achieve state-
of-the-art performance for Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN)
search in practice, yet they often lack theoretical guarantees on
query time or recall due to their reliance on randomized heuristic
constructions. Conversely, existing theoretically grounded struc-
tures are typically difficult to implement and fail to scale in real-
world scenarios.
In this paper, we introduce Hierarchical 𝜀-Net Navigation (HENN),
a property of hierarchical graphs grounded in 𝜀-net theory from
computational geometry. HENN enables formal query-time guar-
antees for ANN search on graphs that satisfy this property and is
agnostic to the choice of proximity graph at each layer, treating it as
a black box. We further show that HNSW satisfies the HENN prop-
erty with high probability, yielding the first formal time guarantees
for HNSW.
Constructing HENN graphs requires finding 𝜀-nets, whose ex-
isting construction methods are either probabilistic or impractical
in high dimensions. To address this, we propose a budget-aware
algorithm that constructs 𝜀-nets under a user-specified preprocess-
ing time budget, enabling efficient HENN construction and index
compression.
Empirical evaluations validate our theoretical guarantees for
both HENN and HNSW. Furthermore, our experiments demon-
strate the effectiveness of the proposed budget-aware algorithm
for constructing HENN and, more generally, 𝜀-nets. This flexibility
allows practitioners to select a method that best fits their specific
use case.
1 Introduction
The Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search problem involves
retrieving the 𝑘closest points to a given query point 𝑞in a𝑑-
dimensional metric space. This problem is foundational in database
systems, machine learning, information retrieval, and computer
vision, and has seen growing importance in large language models
(LLMs) [ 34], particularly in retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
pipelines, where relevant documents must be retrieved efficiently
from large corpora [ 18,39]. More generally, any vector database
system implements some form of ANN search to enable efficient
vector similarity queries [ 4,24,55]. For a comprehensive overview
of additional applications, we refer the reader to the following
surveys [40, 42, 63].
Several classes of algorithms have been developed for ANN.
Hash-based approaches (e.g., Locality-Sensitive Hashing [ 11,26,
Conference’17, Washington, DC, USA
2026. ACM ISBN 978-x-xxxx-xxxx-x/YYYY/MM
https://doi.org/10.1145/nnnnnnn.nnnnnnn29]) offer theoretical guarantees on retrieval quality but often strug-
gle in practical settings [ 57]. Quantization-based methods cluster
data and search among representative centroids, yielding speedups
at the cost of approximation error [ 21,32,54]. Graph-based ap-
proaches [ 31,46,63], particularly hierarchical variants [ 43,46,49],
have gained attention due to their strong empirical performance
and scalability. These methods build graphs over the dataset and
perform greedy traversal to locate approximate neighbors quickly.1
Among them, the Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) [ 46]
graph is widely used in practice [ 8,16,17,48,56,62]. HNSW or-
ganizes data into multiple layers by assigning each point to a ran-
domly chosen level and constructs navigable small-world graphs
at each layer. While HNSW is widely used in many existing tools
and is easy to implement, it lacks formal guarantees on query time,
while the upper-bound on its worst-case complexity is linear to
dataset size inadversarial settings [ 30,58]. In contrast, earlier theo-
retically grounded hierarchical structures like Cover Trees [ 6,36],
provide logarithmic query time guarantees by constructing hierar-
chies using𝑟-nets.2However, these structures are relatively difficult
to implement and often do not scale well to real datasets, limiting
their practical adoption.
In this work, we introduce a general class of hierarchical graphs
for ANN search, grounded in 𝜀-net theory from computational ge-
ometry [ 28]. In these graphs, each layer forms an 𝜀-net of the preced-
ing one, while the choice of proximity graph at each layer remains
agnostic, allowing the use of any suitable graph. The framework is
general enough to include a wide range of graph-based ANN algo-
rithms introduced in the recent literature [ 19,31,32,47,55,59,63];
therefore, we treat such graphs abstractly and focus on their prop-
erties. Our framework establishes time guarantees for such indices
as a function of the properties of the underlying proximity graph.
Notably, we show that HNSW is a special case of HENN with
high probability, corresponding to the choice of NSW as the base
proximity graph. As a crucial building block, we further propose
budget-aware practical algorithms for computing 𝜀-nets on large
datasets, enabling the scalable construction of these hierarchical
graphs and offering a mechanism for compressing these indices by
trading additional preprocessing time for improved space efficiency.
Our contributions can be summarized as follows:
•We introduce a general property for hierarchical indices in ANN
search, called Hierarchical 𝜀-Net Navigation (HENN) , where each
layer is an𝜀-net of the preceding one. This framework is agnostic
to the choice of proximity graph and can be combined with
