common graphs such as kNN, NSW [ 46], NSG [ 19], RNG [ 59], or
other existing ANN graphs [63].
•We formalize a property of proximity graphs, called the recall
bound , which quantifies how well a graph performs when used
within a hierarchical index.
•We establish probabilistic query-time bounds for HENN graphs,
which are logarithmic in the dataset size and parameterized by
the properties of the underlying proximity graph.
•We show that HNSW is a HENN with high probability, when
NSW is used as the proximity graph, thereby yielding formal
probabilistic time guarantees for HNSW .
•A key component in building HENN graphs is computing 𝜀-nets.
Existing randomized algorithms [ 28] provide𝜀-nets only with a
fixed success probability, while deterministic methods based on
discrepancy theory [ 9] are impractical in high dimensions. We
design a budget-aware algorithm that allows the user to specify
a preprocessing budget. More preprocessing time increases the
probability of successfully constructing an 𝜀-net.
•Our budget-aware algorithm leads to the construction of more
compact indexes , effectively yielding a compressed version of
HNSW while preserving recall.
Our empirical evaluation confirms the theoretical time bounds
forHENN andHNSW as a special case of HENN, as well as for other
HENN-based structures. We show that these bounds hold with high
probability (greater than 0.99) in practical scenarios, and further
demonstrate the flexibility of HENN by integrating it with different
proximity graphs and comparing their performance. In addition, we
illustrate how our budget-aware algorithm enables the construction
of compressed indices: by allocating more preprocessing time, one
can reduce index size while maintaining recall, highlighting a clear
tradeoff between preprocessing cost and query performance. Finally,
we implement HENN as a new index in the popular Faiss [ 17]
library and compare it against state-of-the-art baselines, showing
its equivalence to HNSW and empirically validating our theoretical
claims.
Paper Organization . We begin by providing formal definitions
and necessary background on 𝜀-net theory and proximity graphs
(Section 2). In Section 3, we introduce the HENN structure. The sub-
sequent sections establish theoretical bounds (Section 4) and show
that HNSW can be viewed as a HENN graph (Section 5). We thendiscuss practical aspects of constructing HENN graphs, including
algorithms for computing 𝜀-nets in Section 6, followed by our ex-
perimental evaluation in Section 7. The discussions and limitations
are in Section 8. Finally, the related work is discussed in Section 9.
The appendix contains background on different proximity graphs,
results for parallel and dynamic settings, and further experimental
details.
