5 HNSW is a HENN Graph
In this section, we present one of our main contributions: showing
that HNSW is a HENN graph, with a high probability. Building on
this connection, we derive probabilistic time guarantees for HNSW.
We begin with a simplified description of HNSW [ 46]. During
preprocessing, points are inserted incrementally into the index. For
each new point, a random variable determines its maximum layer,
with the probability of assignment to higher layers decreasing ex-
ponentially. After the layers are determined, the point is connected
at each layer to its nearest neighbors among the previously inserted
points, identified using the greedy search procedure (Algorithm 1).
Equivalently, HNSW can be viewed as starting from a base layer
containing all points and recursively constructing higher layers
by randomly sampling subsets of points, such that the size of each
layer decreases exponentially with the level. At each layer, an NSW
graph [ 47] is built on the sampled subset. Comparing this construc-
tion with the definition of HENN,9we observe that HNSW satisfies
the HENN property with high probability, where NSW serves as the
underlying proximity graph and the 𝜀-net construction is realized
through random sampling (cf. Theorem 1).
