The algorithm begins with a small random sample Nfrom the
point set𝑋, similar to General Net-Finder [51]. At each iteration,
it identifies a heavy range𝑅not intersectingN:
𝑅∈R,|𝑅|≥𝜀·|𝑋|, 𝑅∩N=∅.
While General Net-Finder proceeds until all heavy ranges are
covered, Budget-Aware terminates after at most Biterations. The
parameterB, specified by the user, controls the preprocessing time
and introduces a trade-off between efficiency and the final success
probability (see Section 6.3).
Finding an Unhit Range . A naive implementation of the function
FindUnhitRange would require iterating through all ranges 𝑅∈R
and checking their intersection with N, which is impractical. To
address this issue, we use a heuristic strategy for identifying unhit
ring ranges . Specifically, we select a random point 𝑝from the dataset
and partition the remaining points into rings centered at 𝑝, each
containing at least an 𝜀-fraction of the data. We then search for a
ring among these partitions that does not intersect N. Any such
ring serves as a valid unhit range.
Although this heuristic does not guarantee the existence of an
unhit range in all cases, our empirical results indicate that such
rings can consistently be found in practice. As a result, this approach
enables a significantly faster execution of Algorithm 3, avoiding
the need to enumerate all possible ranges.
6.3 Analysis of Budget-Aware Algorithm
We analyze both the running time of the Budget-Aware algorithm
and bound the failure probability as a function of the user-provided
budgetB.
