6.3.1 Index Compression. One advantage of the Budget-Aware al-
gorithm is its ability to compress HENN index and, consequently,
HNSW index. By allocating additional preprocessing budget, the
algorithm constructs an 𝜀-net that satisfies the conditions of Theo-
rem 4 using a smaller sample size while still covering most heavy
ranges. Using fewer sampled points both increases the likelihood
of meeting the 𝜀-net guarantees and reduces the size of the layers.
This effect is validated experimentally in Section 7 and illustrated
in Figure 8.
7 Experiments
In this section, we empirically validate the theoretical results of the
proposed HENN structure and assess the algorithms developed for
its construction. Furthermore, we implement the general HENN
graph within the widely used Faiss library [ 17,35], providing it as a
new index alongside the existing popular ANN indices. The code is
publicly available at this anonymous repository. Additional experi-
mental results are provided in Appendix E due to space constraints.
We organize the experiments into the following parts:
(1)Experiments Setup. Description of datasets (both real and syn-
thetic), baseline methods, evaluation metrics, and configuration
details.
(2)Proximity Graph Integration. Integration of different prox-
imity graphs into the HENN framework and evaluation of their
performance.
(3)Verification of Time Guarantees. Empirical validation of the
time bounds proved in Section 4.
(4)𝜀-net Construction. Comparison of introduced algorithms for
building𝜀-nets (Section 6) and their impact when integrated
into HENN.
(5)Comparison with Other ANN Indices. Evaluation of a stan-
dard implementation of HENN integrated with the HNSW index
in the widely used FAISS library, and comparison against other
indices available there, including LSH [ 11], IVF-PQ [ 32], and
NSG [19].
(6)More on Recall Bound. Comparison of different proximity
