Comparison with Other ANN Indices . We integrated HENN as a
new index within the popular Faiss library and compared it against
widely used ANN indices. Figure 9 presents this comparison. As
expected, HENN+NSW andHNSW exhibit nearly identical perfor-
mance in both recall and query time, consistent with our discussion
in Section 5. The only noticeable difference is a slightly higher
preprocessing time for HENN+NSW , which arises from employ-
ing the construction Algorithm 2 rather than the highly optimized
incremental procedure used in HNSW .12
Analysis of the Recall Bound . In Appendix F, we provide a de-
tailed interpretation of Theorem 4. This result enables a theoretical
characterization of the query-time distribution of HENN as a func-
tion of the recall bound of the underlying proximity graph across
different values of 𝛾.
Figure 23 in Appendix F illustrates the probability mass function
of the output rank for different proximity graphs and datasets, ob-
tained by sampling from this distribution. Graphs with stronger
recall bounds exhibit probability mass concentrated on smaller
ranks. In contrast, more challenging datasets (e.g., Mixture of Gaus-
sians) tend to induce heavier tails, with probability mass peaking
at larger values of 𝑘. This behavior is a consequence of the greedy
search algorithm becoming trapped in local minima. Figure 24 in
Appendix F shows the derived distribution of the HENN query time
(random variable 𝑇) as derived from Theorem 4. Finally, Figure 10
and Figure 11 empirically validate our theoretical predictions: the
12Our objective in this study is not to provide a comprehensive comparison of
HENN against all ANN baselines, but rather to demonstrate the equivalence between
HENN+NSW andHNSW , since HENN represents a structural property rather than a
specific graph.observed query time closely correlates with the recall of the proxim-
ity graph, where stronger recall bounds lead to faster query times,
while weaker recall bounds result in slower query times.
Additional Experiments . Further results on parameter variations,
such as exponential decay, comparisons of recall bounds across
different proximity graphs, index size, and preprocessing time are
provided in Appendix E.
8 Discussion and Future Work
Parallelization and Dynamic Setting . HENN construction can
be naturally parallelized during the indexing phase, enabling faster
preprocessing. Moreover, it can support dynamic updates to the
dataset, provided that the layers are maintained and the 𝜀-net prop-
erty is preserved. In the following, we discuss extensions to dynamic
settings and parallel construction. Further details on parallel con-
struction and dynamic maintenance are provided in Appendices C
and B.
Metrics . HENN is defined as a structural property that extends
to any metric dyielding bounded VC-dimension for ring ranges.
This includes widely used metrics such as ℓ𝑝-norms and angular
distances (cosine similarity). However, if the VC-dimension is un-
bounded,𝜀-net construction becomes impractical.
Recall Bound and Degree . The theoretical query-time guarantees
of HENN depend on key properties of the underlying proximity
graph, namely its recall bound and degree. As established by Theo-
rem 4 and shown by our experimental results, graphs with stronger
recall bounds yield faster query times, whereas graphs with weaker
recall bounds lead to slower performance. The degree of a proximity
